WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.719 --> 00:00:05.919
Collecting plastic waste in rivers and the
ocean is a fantastic thing to do, but it‘s

00:00:05.919 --> 00:00:08.890
only a small part of the problem.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:13.270
TeamSeas is a fundraising project organised
by YouTubers which aims to remove 30 million

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:16.840
pounds of plastic and trash from rivers and
the ocean.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:24.220
But the crazy thing is, globally we release
twice that amount every single day.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:29.550
Now I’m not saying TeamSeas is a bad idea
at all, that'll be 30 million pounds, or 13.6

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.890
thousand tonnes of plastic and garbage that
would otherwise be polluting the environment

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.770
and removing it is fantastic.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:41.540
But we have to put this in perspective with
the estimated 10 million tonnes of plastic

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.120
released each year.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.360
And the fact that removing 30 million pounds
of plastic and garbage is going to cost $30

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.770
million dollars gives us an idea of the scale
of the problem.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:56.540
By this count we’d need to spend $60 million
dollars per day just to deal with plastic

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.250
waste in rivers and oceans.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.720
Who should pay for this?

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:00.840
It shouldn’t be you.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.820
It should be the plastic companies who produce
the plastic in the first place.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:09.690
And they have the money: they’re a $580
billion dollar industry.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.690
But they don’t have to worry about any of
the environmental consequences.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:17.750
They are happy for the environment to be destroyed
as long as they make lots of money.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:22.711
Now one worry I have about teamseas is, if
we’re paying for the plastic cleanup, it

00:01:22.711 --> 00:01:26.880
lets these plastic companies carry on business
as usual.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.890
And collecting trash is really just moving
trash.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.180
Where does it go?

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:36.409
Either to landfill where over time it breaks
down into microplastics, and unfortunately

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:42.060
can end up leaching out back into the water
system anyway, or it gets burned which is

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.640
obviously bad for the environment.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.190
So the real problem here is all the trash
we create in the first place.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.010
So what should we do about it?

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:56.710
Here are 5 practical things we can do to reduce
plastic pollution.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:58.020
Number 1 : regulate.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.280
Plastic pollution is a big social-political
problem that is too big for any single individual

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.650
to solve.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.299
Governments need to regulate plastic production,
and make companies pay for the environmental

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:09.000
damage they cause.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.220
Plastic companies will carry on their harmful
business practices as long as governments

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:13.910
stand by.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:17.900
The best thing you can do is to vote for people
who are strong on the environment.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.890
And let your local representative know that
your vote depends on their actions on the

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.640
environment, and then hold them to account.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.980
We need increased fees and taxes on the production
of plastics to make the plastic producing

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:36.920
companies pay for the environmental costs,
and implement a total ban on single use plastics.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.550
Also we can stop subsidising the oil plastics
are made from which is $20 billion per year

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:48.010
of your money in the US or $500 billion a
year globally.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:51.160
Number 2 : reduce our plastic dependency.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:57.610
Today the average person in the world uses
95lb or 43kg of plastic per year, but people

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:04.360
in the US average at 231lb and people in the
UK 218lb.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:11.980
So if say, you donate $10 to team seas, this
corresponds to 10lb of plastic removed which

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:17.099
is only about 4% of your yearly plastic usage
if you are the average person.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:20.680
So a great thing to do is to try and reduce
our own plastic use.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:26.160
But we can’t blame ourselves too much - plastic
is everywhere and it’s hard to avoid.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.989
But wherever possible we should avoid and
refuse single use plastics.

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:34.450
The less plastic we use, the less plastic
is wasted and the less plastic will need to

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.920
be produced in the first place.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.110
You can re-use a lot of plastic yourself.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:43.069
Next time you get a plastic fork from the
takeout, wash it and keep it in your bag and

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:45.769
you can reuse it over and over again.

00:03:45.769 --> 00:03:47.510
You can bring your own bags to do shopping.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:52.350
And bring a stainless steel water bottle rather
than buying drinks in disposable bottles.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:57.409
And, as an added bonus, you’ll also avoid
the bad health impacts from drinking microplastics

00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:00.120
which are known to leech off plastic bottles.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.830
Also, there are more and more plastic free
grocery stores and refill shops popping up,

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:08.930
where you can bring your empty plastic containers
and get them refilled.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.080
So why not see if there is one in your area.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:16.430
Re-using plastic is much better than recycling
which brings us to number 3: Don’t think

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.139
recycling is the answer.

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:23.480
These aren’t recycling symbols, these are
symbols made by plastic companies to make

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.670
us think they’re recycling symbols.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:30.560
They’re actually symbols to say what kind
of plastic this is, and most of these numbers

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.460
are not actually recyclable.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:38.169
Recycling was a crafty way for the big plastic
producers to make us feel guilty about plastic

00:04:38.169 --> 00:04:43.230
use, and distract us from the fact that they
are actually the people actually causing the

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.940
problem.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:51.240
Only 10% of plastic has ever been recycled,
and then mostly it can only be recycled once.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:52.889
What’s the solution?

00:04:52.889 --> 00:04:57.451
Well keep on recycling, because it is still
better than nothing, but again, the best thing

00:04:57.451 --> 00:05:01.860
is to not use single-use plastics in the first
place.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.250
Number 4 : Educate ourselves.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.310
The best thing we can do is educate ourselves
as much as possible.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:11.030
I’ve recently learned that a great way for
the world to stop plastics from ending up

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:16.130
in the ocean is to improve waste management
where the problem is the greatest.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:20.650
90% of ocean plastic comes from just 10 rivers.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.930
These are places that don’t have the infrastructure
to deal with the plastic waste.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:29.370
So globally we need to invest in waste management
in these areas to collect plastic before it

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.850
even reaches the rivers and oceans.

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:37.770
And, we just need a plan: international agreements
with firm targets and time frames for reducing

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:41.920
plastics and cleanup, just like we do for
carbon emissions.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.690
Really this is the only way the problem will
properly be solved.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.310
Clean up plastic.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:48.390
5: Cleanup.

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.919
Finally we can actively help solve the problem
ourselves.

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:56.050
A lot of the plastic in the ocean ends up
getting washed up on the shorelines.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:59.840
So the next time you go to a beach, bring
a bag to collect any rubbish you find.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.090
Joining a local shoreline cleanup is another
great way to feel good about actively making

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:05.840
things better.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.600
But like I’ve said, the problem is way bigger
than anything we can solve on our own.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:14.380
Globally there needs to be increased funds
for cleanup operations where the problem is

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:15.380
greatest.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:20.100
Again, this relies on governments to work
together to establish a global ocean fund

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.080
with waste management and clean-up of marine
areas high on the agenda.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:26.560
This is where teamseas will help.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.900
Half of the money will go to The Ocean Conservancy
and their International Coastal Cleanup which

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:35.150
both have excellent track records of work.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:38.949
And the other half of the money will go to
The Ocean Cleanup which, I think it’s fair

00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:42.480
to say, has a pretty poor track record to
date.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.640
But apparently things will be different this
time?

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.580
I dunno, it’s a bit of a question mark still
for me to be honest but I’d recommend you

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.009
doing your own research to see what you think.

00:06:52.009 --> 00:06:55.120
So if you’d like to donate you can donate
to team seas.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:59.210
Or if you’d like to choose exactly where
your money is going, you can also donate directly

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:04.039
to The Ocean Conservancy, another great option
is River Cleanup.

00:07:04.039 --> 00:07:08.770
Or if you want to focus on the protection
of marine life, Oceana is a fantastic choice

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:11.120
as well as the world wildlife fund.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.280
Links to all these are in the description
below.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:19.819
I love the oceans and all the amazing creatures
that live there, so much so that me and a

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:24.009
friend made a whole book about them, and I
think it’s great this movement is raising

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:26.490
awareness of plastic pollution.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:31.520
And although as we’ve seen there are many
significant problems to solve, ocean plastics

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:33.699
is a solvable problem.

00:07:33.699 --> 00:07:38.210
According to research we have the solutions
today that could reduce the flow of plastics

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:44.590
into the oceans by 80% by 2040, and a lot
more cost effectively than the $60 million

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:50.250
dollars per day figure I mentioned earlier,
we just need governments, corporations and

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:53.210
investors to back these plans.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:57.550
But on a personal level the best things you
can do are to stay educated, put pressure

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:01.930
on your local government officials, and do
whatever it takes to make sure the plastic

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:07.370
producing companies make as little money from
you as possible by refusing to use single

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:08.620
use plastics.

